<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taiwan Relations Act</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1979">1979</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> Relations Act is a law passed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-congress">United States Congress</a> in April 1979, after Washington switched diplomatic recognition from the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to the People's Republic. It maintained commercial, cultural and other ties with Taiwan through the American Institute in Taiwan and committed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to provide Taiwan with arms of a defensive character.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> became paramount leader at the Third Plenum in 1978, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> named the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> rather than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> as its main security threat and established relations with the United States, which in exchange ended its mutual defence treaty with the Republic of China, signed in December 1954. The ROC government mobilised the China Lobby to press Congress for a security guarantee. Senator Barry Goldwater and others challenged President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimmy-carter">Jimmy Carter</a>'s right to end the treaty without the Senate, but their case, Goldwater v. Carter, was dismissed as non-justiciable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Congress rejected the State Department's draft and wrote its own language. Enacted on 10 April 1979, the Act took effect retroactively from 1 January 1979. It speaks of the governing authorities on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> rather than the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and defines Taiwan as the island and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Pescadores</a> (Penghu), leaving out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinmen">Kinmen</a> and the Matsus. It gives the American Institute in Taiwan powers that make it a de facto embassy, keeps earlier agreements in force unless terminated, and has Taiwan treated under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> law like foreign countries and governments.</p><p>The Act neither guarantees nor rules out American military intervention. It commits the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to make defence articles and services available for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>'s self-defense, in amounts the President and Congress decide, and to consider any effort to decide Taiwan's future by other than peaceful means, including boycotts or embargoes, a matter of grave concern. This policy has been called strategic ambiguity, designed to dissuade Taiwan from a unilateral declaration of independence and the People's Republic from unilaterally unifying Taiwan with it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Its passage led Deng to see the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> as an insincere partner, and the People's Republic considers the Act illegal and invalid; on 10 April 2024 Zhu Fenglian of the Taiwan Affairs Office said it seriously violated the one-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> principle. In the communiqué of 17 August 1982 the United States agreed to reduce arms sales, while the Six Assurances of the same year said it would not formally recognise Chinese sovereignty over Taiwan.</p><p>In the late 1990s Congress passed, and Bill Clinton signed, a non-binding resolution giving the Act greater weight than the three communiqués, and a Congressional Research Service report of July 2007 confirmed that American policy had not recognised the People's Republic's sovereignty over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. On 19 May 2016, a day before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsai-ing-wen">Tsai Ing-wen</a> took office, Senators Marco Rubio and Bob Menendez introduced a resolution reaffirming the Act and the Six Assurances.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taiwan Relations Act” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Most states, including Japan in 1972 and the United States in 1979, then switched recognition to Beijing, although the United States kept unofficial ties and arms sales under the <b>Taiwan Relations Act</b> of 1979.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
